# UMASK(2)

## NAME

umask - set file creation mode mask

## SYNOPSIS

```c
int umask(int complmode);
```

## DESCRIPTION

*Umask* sets a mask used whenever a file is created by creat(2) or mknod(2): the actual mode
(see chmod(2)) of the newly-created file is the logical **and** of the given mode and the
complement of the argument. Only the low-order 9 bits of the mask (the protection bits)
participate. In other words, the mask shows the bits to be turned off when files are created.

The previous value of the mask is returned by the call. The value is initially 0 (no
restrictions). The mask is inherited by child processes.

## SEE ALSO

creat(2), mknod(2), chmod(2)

## ASSEMBLER

**Note:** v7's page gave the PDP-11 form here - a decimal call number and a `sys name; arg ...`
line. Neither is how a call is made on this machine. The gate is one extracode - **$77
SYS_umask** - and the C calling convention has already put the arguments where it reads them:
1..n-1 below r15, the last in the accumulator, the result in the accumulator, the error number
in r14.

**The numbering itself is v7's, unchanged.** The macro is defined in <sys/syscall.h>, which is
the only place a syscall number appears on the user side. See intro(2) for the whole contract.
